  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1018 PM MDT Tue May 26 2026\n\nHOT, DRY, AND WINDY CONDITIONS RETURN\n\nTonight, our region is caught between two streams of moisture. \nDense stratus lingers along the Divide along the front edge of \nthe stalled low to our south, and spotty showers are struggling \nto persist off to our west in Utah. Little additional \nprecipitation is expected across the region though, and over the\ncourse of the next 24 hours, drier flow will filter into the \nCWA, cutting off the moisture supply. This comes on the leading \nedge of a deep closed low which has transited over the Great \nBasin as of this evening. This will kick off a three-day \nstretch of critical fire weather conditions for portions of the \nregion, primarily focused on eastern Utah and the Four Corners \nregion. Models have not been consistent in the movement (or lack\nthereof) of the low, leading to some uncertainty regarding if \nwest-central and northwestern Colorado will see critical fire \nweather conditions on Thursday or Friday. Either way though, \nthe low budges as we head into the weekend, bringing a quick \nbout of cooler air and weaker winds, effectively diminishing the\nfire threat.",
